# Choose ROS2 as Robot Middleware

## Context and Problem Statement
We need a communication and control framework for the simulated (and later physical) TurtleBot3 robot. The system must support distributed nodes, standard robot message types, Gazebo integration, and expandability for vision/voice modules.

## Decision Drivers
* Must integrate with TurtleBot3 simulation tools
* Must support multi-node communication
* Needs strong Python support (gesture server)
* Should be compatible with real robot hardware

## Considered Options
* ROS2 Humble
* ROS1 Noetic
* Custom TCP/UDP-based framework

## Decision Outcome
Chosen option: **ROS2 Humble**, because it natively supports TurtleBot3, has DDS-based communication, enables modular nodes, and has direct Gazebo integration.

### Consequences
* Good, because we get standard topics like `/cmd_vel`, `/scan`, and built-in message types.
* Good, because the same code can later run on the real robot.
* Bad, because ROS2 requires learning curve and environment setup.

## Pros and Cons of Options

### ROS2 Humble
* Good: Native TurtleBot3 + Gazebo support  
* Good: Modern middleware (DDS)  
* Good: Python-friendly (rclpy)  
* Bad: Setup complexity on new systems  

### ROS1
* Good: Mature ecosystem  
* Bad: Deprecated and not recommended for new projects

### Custom Networking
* Good: Full control  
* Bad: Recreates features ROS2 already solves  
